{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"10.490 Integrated Chemical Engineering I was taught Fall 2006 as an undergraduate course.","source_span":"10.490 | Fall 2006 | Undergraduate"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"Lectures for 10.490 meet 4 sessions per week, 1 hour per session.","source_span":"Lectures: 4 sessions / week, 1 hour / session"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"The prerequisites for 10.490 are 10.37, 10.32, 10.301, 10.213 and 5.60.","source_span":"The prerequisites for this course include 10.37,\n10.32\n, 10.301, 10.213 and\n5.60"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"A recommended text is King, C. J., Separation Processes, 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1980, ISBN 9780070346123.","source_span":"King, C. J.\nSeparation Processes\n. 2nd ed. New York, NY: McGraw-Hill, 1980. ISBN: 9780070346123."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"A recommended text is Smith, Van Ness, and Abbott, Introduction to Chemical Engineering Thermodynamics, 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2004, ISBN 9780073104454.","source_span":"Smith, J. M., H. C. Van Ness, and M. M. Abbott.\nIntroduction to Chemical Engineering Thermodynamics\n. 7th ed. Boston, MA: McGraw-Hill, 2004. ISBN: 9780073104454."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"A recommended text is Fogler, H. S., Elements of Chemical Reaction Engineering, 4th ed., Prentice Hall PTR, 2005, ISBN 9780130473943.","source_span":"Fogler, H. S.\nElements of Chemical Reaction Engineering\n. 4th ed. Upper Saddle River, NJ: Prentice Hall PTR, 2005. ISBN: 9780130473943."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"Late work extensions in 10.490 cost 10 percent for each 24 hours beyond the deadline, up to a maximum of 30 percent.","source_span":"Extensions cost 10 percent for each 24 hours beyond the deadline, up to a maximum of 30 percent."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"The grading breakdown for 10.490 is Homework 40% and Final Report 60%.","source_span":"Homework\n40%\nFinal Report\n60%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"ABACUSS II is an equation-based process simulator developed at MIT that can solve both dynamic and steady-state problems.","source_span":"ABACUSS II is an equation-based process simulator that has been developed at MIT. ABACUSS II can solve dynamic as well as steady-state problems."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"If students are discovered referencing homework or final project solutions from earlier years, they receive no credit for the assignment in question.","source_span":"If it is discovered that reference has been made to earlier years, the students involved will receive no credit for the assignment in question."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"In the 10.490 course calendar, Homework 1 is due in session L6, Homework 2 is due at L10, Homework 3 is due at L13, and the final report is due at L22.","source_span":"Homework 1 due in Ses #L6 ... Homework 2 due ... Homework 3 due ... Final report due"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"Who is the instructor of MIT's 10.490 Integrated Chemical Engineering I course, and when was it taught?","answer":"Dr. Barry S. Johnston taught 10.490 Integrated Chemical Engineering I in Fall 2006, as an undergraduate course."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are the prerequisites for 10.490 Integrated Chemical Engineering I?","answer":"The prerequisites are 10.37, 10.32, 10.301, 10.213, and 5.60."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[9],"question":"How is the final grade weighted in 10.490 Integrated Chemical Engineering I?","answer":"Homework counts for 40% of the grade and the Final Report counts for 60%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What is the penalty for submitting work late in 10.490, and is there a cap?","answer":"Extensions cost 10 percent for each 24 hours beyond the deadline, up to a maximum penalty of 30 percent."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What is ABACUSS II and where was it developed?","answer":"ABACUSS II is an equation-based process simulator developed at MIT that can solve both dynamic and steady-state problems, such as modeling batch reactor charging, reactions, and valve shutting."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What happens to students in 10.490 who are caught referencing homework or project solutions from previous years?","answer":"They receive no credit for the assignment in question."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-490-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[4,5,6],"question":"What textbooks are recommended for 10.490 Integrated Chemical Engineering I?","answer":"Recommended texts are King's Separation Processes (2nd ed., 1980), Smith/Van Ness/Abbott's Introduction to Chemical Engineering Thermodynamics (7th ed., 2004), and Fogler's Elements of Chemical Reaction Engineering (4th ed., 2005)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","statement":"The instructor for 10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II is Prof. Gregory McRae.","source_span":"Instructor\nProf. Gregory McRae"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","statement":"10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II was taught Spring 2006 as an undergraduate course.","source_span":"10.491 | Spring 2006 | Undergraduate"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","statement":"There are 6 problem sets in 10.491 covering the units in class.","source_span":"There are 6 problem sets covering the units in class."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","statement":"Problem Set 1 in 10.491 covers Order of Magnitude Estimation, Energy to Generate Electricity and to Separate CO2 from a Gas Stream, and A First Order Model of a Power Plant.","source_span":"Order of Magnitude Estimation; Energy to Generate Electricity and to Separate CO\n2\nfrom a Gas Stream; A First Order Model of a Power Plant"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","statement":"Although students are encouraged to work in teams on problem sets, the final solution submitted must be the student's own work.","source_span":"We strongly encourage students to work in teams. The final solution must be your own work."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","statement":"In 10.491, design problem answers are typically not unique and there is no single 'correct answer'.","source_span":"usually there is no ‘correct answer’ nor is the answer in the back of the book"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"Who teaches 10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II and in what term?","answer":"Prof. Gregory McRae teaches 10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II, taught in Spring 2006."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How many problem sets are assigned in 10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II?","answer":"There are 6 problem sets covering the units in class."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What topics does Problem Set 1 cover in 10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II?","answer":"Problem Set 1 covers order of magnitude estimation, the energy needed to generate electricity and to separate CO2 from a gas stream, and a first order model of a power plant."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","fact_refs":[4],"question":"In 10.491, can students collaborate on problem sets, and what is required of the final submission?","answer":"Students are strongly encouraged to work in teams, but the final solution submitted must be their own individual work."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_assignments","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Why are design problem solutions in 10.491 considered especially challenging to grade?","answer":"Because design answers are typically not unique — there is no single 'correct answer,' and the process is further complicated by missing or conflicting information and ambiguous objectives."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II requires that a student has taken 10.490 Integrated Chemical Engineering I as a prerequisite.","source_span":"This course is a senior design course and requires that a student has taken 10.490 Integrated Chemical Engineering I."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"10.491 lectures meet 4 sessions per week, 1 hour per session, with two identical sessions from 10-11 and 11-12.","source_span":"Lectures: 4 sessions / week, 1 hour / session ... There are two class sessions one from 10-11 and another 11-12."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"After three weeks in 10.491, the Friday class session is used for meetings of each student group with the instructor.","source_span":"After three weeks the Friday class will be used for meetings of each student group and with the instructor."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"There are approximately 6 homework sets in 10.491, due approximately one week after being issued, during the beginning of the semester.","source_span":"There will be approximately 6 homework sets during the beginning of the semester. The homework sets will be due approximately one week after being issued."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"Students in 10.491 are formed into teams of four to work on a design project, and each team writes a design report and gives a 30-minute presentation to the class and clients.","source_span":"you will be formed into teams of four students to work on a design project ... Each team will write a design report and give a 30-minute presentation to the class and clients."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"The 10.491 course grade is based on 200 points, with Design Report 75%, Problem Sets 12.5%, and Personal Evaluation 12.5%.","source_span":"The course grade is based on 200 points. ... Design Report\n75%\nProblem Sets\n12.5%\nPersonal Evaluation\n12.5%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"No late design reports are accepted in 10.491.","source_span":"No late reports will be accepted"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"The Personal Evaluation grade in 10.491 is composed of two equal parts: an instructor evaluation of performance in weekly design meetings, and an evaluation by teammates.","source_span":"The Personal Evaluation grade is a subjective evaluation of each group member and will be composed to two equal parts:\nAn evaluation by the instructor of performance in the weekly design meetings.\nAn evaluation by your team mates."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"In the 10.491 calendar, the final report is due in Lecture #26 and Problem Set 1 is due in Lecture #15.","source_span":"Problem set 1 due in Lec #15\n...\nFinal report due in Lec #26"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","statement":"Two optional reference textbooks for 10.491 are Douglas's Conceptual Design of Chemical Processes (1988, ISBN 9780070177628) and Seider, Seader, and Lewin's Product and Process Design Principles (2nd ed., 2003, ISBN 9780471216636).","source_span":"Douglas, J.\nConceptual Design of Chemical Processes\n. New York, NY: McGraw-Hill Science/Engineering/Math, 1988. ISBN: 9780070177628.\nSeider, W. D., J. D. Seader, and D. R. Lewin.\nProduct and Process Design Principles: Synthesis, Analysis, and Evaluation\n. 2nd ed. New York, NY: Wiley, 2003. ISBN: 9780471216636."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What course must a student have taken before enrolling in 10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II?","answer":"Students must have taken 10.490 Integrated Chemical Engineering I, since 10.491 is a senior design course requiring it as a prerequisite."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How large are the design project teams in 10.491, and how long is each team's presentation?","answer":"Teams consist of four students, and each team gives a 30-minute design presentation to the class and clients."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How is the overall course grade broken down in 10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II?","answer":"The course grade is based on 200 points, split into Design Report (75%), Problem Sets (12.5%), and Personal Evaluation (12.5%)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the policy on late design reports in 10.491?","answer":"No late reports will be accepted."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How is a student's Personal Evaluation grade determined in 10.491?","answer":"It is a subjective evaluation composed of two equal parts: an instructor evaluation of performance in weekly design meetings, and an evaluation from teammates."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[8],"question":"In the 10.491 course calendar, in which lecture is the final report due?","answer":"The final report is due in Lecture #26."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-491-integrated-chemical-engineering-i_syllabus","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What are two optional reference textbooks suggested for 10.491 Integrated Chemical Engineering II?","answer":"Douglas's Conceptual Design of Chemical Processes (1988) and Seider, Seader, and Lewin's Product and Process Design Principles, 2nd ed. (2003) are suggested as useful references, though no text is required."}
